Guide to Flashing and Unlocking the BIOS (1vyrain)

Part 1: Downgrade / Flash the Stock BIOS

1. Create a Bootable MS-DOS USB: Download Rufus and use it to create a bootable MS-DOS (or FreeDOS) USB flash drive. (https://github.com/pbatard/rufus/releases)
2. Copy the BIOS Files: Copy the bios260 folder (found inside the "only file require to flash bios" directory) onto the root of your MS-DOS USB drive.
3. Enter BIOS Setup: Turn on or reboot your laptop and immediately press F1 to enter the BIOS settings.
4. Configure BIOS Settings: Adjust the settings as suggested in this GitHub repository:
	• Go to the Startup tab and make sure UEFI/Legacy Boot is set to Both.
	• Set the UEFI BIOS Update Option exactly as shown in the README section of this project page.
	• Press F10 to save and exit. As soon as text appears on the screen during reboot, press F12 repeatedly to open the Boot Menu.
	• Select your USB drive from the boot picker and press Enter.
5. Flash the BIOS in DOS: Once you reach the MS-DOS command prompt, type the following commands one by one and press Enter after each:dos
dir
cd bios260
dosflash /sd /file bios.fl1

Use code with caution.
(Note: dir lists the directories, cd bios260 opens the folder containing the flash files, and dosflash flashes the BIOS chip.)
6. Wait for Completion (Critical Warning):
	• Do not press any keys during the flashing process.
	• Do not disconnect your computer from the power source. Losing power mid-flash will brick your BIOS chip. Recovering a bricked chip requires a CH341A EEPROM programmer or a Raspberry Pi, which involves desoldering the chip from the motherboard, manually flashing the bios.fl1 file, and resoldering it.
	• Hardware Warning: Always verify the pin voltage before connecting a BIOS chip—some CH341A programmers output the wrong voltage (5V instead of 3.3V) and can permanently destroy the chip. Always read the chip's datasheet carefully before taking action.
7. Reboot: Once the flash finishes, you will see the Lenovo logo or POST text depending on your BIOS settings. Press F1 to enter the BIOS and verify your settings.

Part 2: Create and Run the 1vyrain Bootable USB

Step 1: Create the 1vyrain USB Drive

• For Windows Users: Use balenaEtcher to flash the 1vyrain.iso image onto your USB drive.
• For Linux Users:
	1. Identify your USB drive letter (e.g., sdb or sdc) by running
sudo fdisk -l

	2. Convert the USB drive to a GPT partition scheme using gdisk or GParted. To use gdisk, run (replace X with your actual drive letter)
sudo gdisk /dev/sdX

Inside gdisk, enter the following commands in order:
		• o (creates a new, empty GPT table and removes the old partition layout)
		• y (confirms the action)
		• w (writes the changes to the disk)
		• y (confirms writing to the disk)
	3. Write the 1vyrain.iso image to the USB drive using dd (replace X with your drive letter, and feel free to drag and drop the .iso file into the terminal after if=)
sudo dd if=1vyrain.iso of=/dev/sdX bs=4M status=progress conv=fsync

Step 2: Run the 1vyrain Exploit

1. Reboot your PC, press F12, select your USB drive, and boot from it.
2. Follow the on-screen instructions. When the laptop enters deep sleep mode, press the Power button to wake the system up and continue following the prompts until you see "Choose your option".
3. Select Option 1 and press Enter.
	• If it gives an error: Press Enter multiple times or wait a moment for the computer to automatically reboot.
	• If Option 1 is unsuccessful: Repeat the boot and sleep/wake steps until the "Choose your option" menu appears again, then select Option 2 and press Enter.

Part 3: Final BIOS Configuration (macOS / Hackintosh Setup)

Once the exploit completes, your computer will reboot and the BIOS settings will reset to factory defaults. Press F1 to enter the BIOS and configure the following:
1. Re-apply the general BIOS settings recommended in this repository's guide.
2. Go to the Advanced section and adjust the graphics settings as specified in the README.
3. Intel Management Engine (ME): Keep this Enabled (default) if you want graphics acceleration to work in macOS.
4. Intel AMT: Do not try to disable Intel AMT 1vyrain exploit relies on it, and it will automatically re-enable on the next reboot anyway.
5. Intel Rapid Start Technology: Make sure to Disable this feature.
Well done! You have successfully unlocked your BIOS.
